course: dictionary
lesson: 3205
title: What English means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: English is a language, the one you are reading right now. It is spoken in many countries, such as the USA, the UK, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland. «English lessons help us read and write better.» Here English is a school subject.
explain: English can also describe things. «The English countryside has green fields and hedges.» Here English means coming from England. The word after English is a clue: countryside is a place, so English describes it.
explain: Use the words around English to decide which meaning fits. If the sentence is about reading, writing, stories or poems, it means the subject or the language. If it describes a place or a person from there, it means coming from England.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably picked the right one.
fact: English is a language, and it is spoken in many countries around the world.
fact: When English describes something, it means coming from England.
fact: British Sign Language (BSL) is a full language with its own grammar, different from English.
fact: J, U and W were added to English only in the last thousand years.
fact: For most English speakers «which» and «witch» sound the same, but many speakers in Scotland and Ireland say «wh» with a breathy sound.
exercise: «The English countryside has green fields and hedges.» What does English mean here? | A town in Scotland | *Coming from England | A school subject where you learn to read and write | The language you are reading now
exercise: «English lessons help us read and write better.» What is English in this sentence? | The people of Canada | *A school subject where you learn to read and write well | Coming from England | A kind of sign language
exercise: Which letters were added to English only in the last thousand years? | A, B and C | Q, X and Z | *J, U and W | Å, Ä and Ö
test: «For most English speakers "which" and "witch" sound the same.» Which speakers say them differently? | People in the USA only | People who use British Sign Language | People who have never heard English | *People in Scotland and Ireland, who say «wh» with a breathy sound
test: «John Locke, an English philosopher of the 1600s, said people make things their own by mixing their work with them.» What does English mean in this sentence? | The subject you study at school | *Coming from England | A sign language with its own grammar | Very old
exam: «Calling someone stupid is rude, so English has dozens of jokey ways to say it, such as "a few sandwiches short of a picnic".» What does this phrase suggest? | *A person who is not very clever, as if their head is missing a few pieces | A very happy day out | A new sign in British Sign Language | A picnic with missing food
